[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:15] Okay. You've worked that through from the cost base. I don't have price elasticity and competitor pricing. Setting pricing aside for a moment: beyond a price increase, what else could Brew & Bean do to rebuild its margin?

[candidate · 1:22] A few other levers, in rough order of how much I'd trust them.

First, procurement. Beans at +40% is the market, but they may be buying spot. Forward contracts or hedging on green coffee, and renegotiating dairy and packaging at 200-store scale, could take some of that back. That's cost-side with no demand risk.

Second, mix. Rather than raising the headline latte price, push higher-margin items — food attachment, larger sizes, cold brew — and lift ticket without changing posted prices. Also reformulation: cheaper milk alternatives, lighter cup stock, portion control on dairy.

Third, overhead. It's up 2 points on 15% more revenue, which means corporate costs grew faster than the business. That's worth 2 points and is the easiest ask.

Fourth, labor is 22% and flat, so it's not the cause, but there may be scheduling efficiency available.

Honestly though, the arithmetic says pricing has to do most of the work — 38% input inflation can't be absorbed by procurement alone.
